The DC node reports status "passed" because its postconditions check that
synthesis completed, not that timing closed -- so the miss is visible only in
the qor report and in results.json, where violating_paths is 80. Worth knowing
about the instrument: a green node is not a closed design.

How to state it: **Gemmini's RTL was not targeted at this constraint.** Not
"Gemmini is slower". The miss is -0.01 ns against a 3.33 ns target, 0.3% and
80 paths out of a 491,512-cell design, which any of a longer period, a higher
effort or a synthesis retarget would absorb. Nothing here supports a frequency
claim in either direction, and a DC topographical estimate is a floor rather
than an achieved Fmax in the first place.

Read beside its logic-only twin (524,401.97): the stubbed scratchpad and
accumulator account for 53.9% of this figure. And beside DIM=4 full
(990,938.03): +14.9% for doubling DIM, against +37.3% on the logic-only pair --
the full-design delta is smaller because the flip-flop memories are a fixed
cost at both sizes and they dominate. That is the clearest case in the set for
why the logic-only pair is the one to quote: the same design change reads as
+14.9% or +37.3% depending only on how the memories were treated.
